WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
What if the neurons are made, but never arrive?

2
00:00:06.883 --> 00:00:11.843
In classic lissencephaly, the cortex is thick and nearly smooth, with about four

3
00:00:11.843 --> 00:00:12.843
crude layers.

4
00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:18.303
Yet in isolated lissencephaly, the head is usually near normal in size at birth.

5
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:24.773
Children with Miller-Dieker syndrome lack a piece of one chromosome 17, including

6
00:00:24.773 --> 00:00:25.913
LIS1.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:31.203
Losing only one copy of LIS1 still causes lissencephaly.

8
00:00:32.123 --> 00:00:33.203
Half a dose is too little.

9
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.507
A mould gave the clue.

10
00:00:36.407 --> 00:00:42.387
Its relative of LIS1 helps space nuclei, as do parts of dynein, a microtubule motor.

11
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.480
In mammalian cells, LIS1 binds dynein and helps it work.

12
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.760
A migrating neuron moves in two steps.

13
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.480
First the leading process extends, and the centrosome moves into it.

14
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.160
Then dynein pulls the nucleus up toward the centrosome.

15
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:05.003
Predict. Knock down LIS1 in the embryonic rat cortex.

16
00:01:05.963 --> 00:01:08.723
Does the process fail, the nucleus, or something earlier?

17
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:18.203
Most cells never started, and stayed many-branched.

18
00:01:18.903 --> 00:01:23.003
In the few that did, the process kept growing, but the nucleus stayed behind.

19
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:30.187
The knockdown also blocked progenitor divisions at the ventricle.

20
00:01:30.847 --> 00:01:33.027
So LIS1 has one foot in too few.

21
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.370
A second gene makes doublecortin.

22
00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:39.190
It is not a motor.

23
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.290
It binds microtubules, and is thought mainly to steady the tracks.

24
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:49.430
Now the puzzle. A boy and his mother share a mutation that breaks doublecortin.

25
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.710
He has a smooth brain; she has a second band.

26
00:01:54.883 --> 00:02:00.843
The clue: a male has one X. Each of a female's cells silences one of her two X

27
00:02:00.843 --> 00:02:02.003
chromosomes at random.

28
00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:05.793
Predict. Explain both brains.

29
00:02:12.283 --> 00:02:16.823
His one X carries the mutation, so no neuron makes working doublecortin.

30
00:02:17.643 --> 00:02:19.483
Most stall, and the cortex is smooth.

31
00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:23.273
Her cortex is a mosaic.

32
00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:26.293
Neurons with the normal X active arrive.

33
00:02:27.153 --> 00:02:31.733
Those with the mutant X active stall partway, and gather into a band beneath the

34
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:32.733
cortex.

35
00:02:33.217 --> 00:02:38.297
In embryonic rats, neurons with doublecortin silenced stalled, and formed such a

36
00:02:38.297 --> 00:02:39.297
band.

37
00:02:39.567 --> 00:02:44.127
Some neighbours were disturbed too, and the sorting has not been traced neuron by

38
00:02:44.127 --> 00:02:45.127
neuron in humans.

39
00:02:46.017 --> 00:02:47.757
So read a cortex two ways.

40
00:02:48.837 --> 00:02:49.837
Size counts the doublings.

41
00:02:50.797 --> 00:02:51.917
Layering records the journey.
